From cf4d70055c6ae34c550cf38be4e8a71e61235262 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:08:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dark mode: draw a darkened page by the role each colour plays

Forced dark mode inverted the lightness of every colour it chose to
change, so a darkened page looked inverted rather than drawn dark. A
background was inverted above a brightness of 205 and kept at or below
it, so #d0d0d0 turned dark beside a #cccccc that stayed light, and
white became #121212 whatever the application around the page looked
like.

Blink already hands the filter each colour's role. A fifth inversion
algorithm, InversionAlgorithm=4 in --dark-mode-settings, maps each
colour by that role with Dark Reader's colour functions (MIT licensed,
notice carried in dark_mode_color_filter.cc): backgrounds, selection
and gradient stops toward a ground colour, text and list markers
toward a text colour, borders onto a mid to dark range. SVG fills,
strokes and text share one role, so a light SVG colour takes the
background mapping and a dark one the text mapping: measured on
Wikipedia, its dark wordmark went dark on the dark ground under the
background mapping alone. A near grey takes the hue of the colour it
is drawn toward. Every background is mapped, with no threshold, and a
dark one stays dark. Text is held at 4.5:1 against its own background,
or the ground where it has none, measured on the colour as drawn in 8
bits. A colour is read in 8 bits a channel, as Dark Reader reads it,
so oklch(1 0 0), which arrives with float noise, is white and not a
saturated cyan. The inverted colour cache is keyed on colour and role.
Images keep the classifier and the inversion they had.

The two colours are each page's, not the process's.
QWebEngineSettings::setForceDarkModeGroundColor and
setForceDarkModeTextColor, and forceDarkModeGroundColor and
forceDarkModeTextColor on WebEngineSettings in Qt Quick, reach Blink's
settings through WebPreferences. A GraphicsContext painting a frame
takes them from its page's settings, and a change repaints a page
already showing, with no reload. An invalid colour is the parent
settings', and unset they are Dark Reader's #181a1b and #e8e6e3. The
other algorithms ignore them.
